WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
When a physical skyscraper goes up, specific structural load points determine how tall the

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.720
building can safely get before it collapses.

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.680
As a React Native application scales beyond a handful of screens, your state management

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.120
decisions bear the weight of your entire architecture.

5
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.080
At the absolute baseline you have used state, its local, invisible to the rest of the application,

6
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.920
and works perfectly for isolated data like a text input or a toggle switch.

7
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.120
This flow chart tracks the first major failure point of local state, prop drilling.

8
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.440
The exact moment you have to pass the same piece of data down through a third component

9
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.280
layer, the friction begins to drag on your performance.

10
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.320
That friction multiplies when two completely unrelated screens need to react to the exact

11
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.120
same piece of data simultaneously.

12
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.520
Local states simply cannot bridge that gap.

13
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.880
Finally, top-level components begin recalculating expensive subtrees on every minor update.

14
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.920
And your data gets permanently wiped out when navigation transitions unmount to screen.

15
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.120
You are forced to look outward for a global container.

16
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.440
The React ecosystem has dozens of competing tools to solve this problem.

17
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.320
If you pick the wrong one for your specific scale, your code base will either suffocate under heavy

18
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.640
boilerplate or devolve into an untraceable mess of spaghetti code.

19
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.000
To prevent long-term technical debt, we need to map which exact state tools belong to which

20
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.240
exact scale of application.

21
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.880
The most significant architectural unlock comes before you ever install a library.

22
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.040
You must explicitly separate your data into two distinct categories.

23
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.720
First is client state.

24
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.400
This is data your app completely owns and controls in memory.

25
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.360
Things like a modal being open, draft form values, or a user's local navigation history.

26
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.360
This matrix highlights the divide.

27
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.520
On the right is server state.

28
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.360
Remote data temporarily cached, like order history.

29
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.800
Forcing this data, which needs background refreshing, into the same container as local UI

30
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:05.160
toggles creates a conflict. One lifecycle is predictable, the other a synchronous.

31
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:11.320
Separating them keeps the application logic clean, but it introduces a specific trade-off.

32
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.000
Your developers now have to learn and maintain two distinct libraries.

33
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.600
To manage the complex lifecycle of remote data,

34
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.480
TanStack Query is the purpose-built standard.

35
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.800
If two different components ask for the exact same data at the same time,

36
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.160
TanStack Query automatically de-duplicates the request.

37
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.000
The network call only happens once.

38
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.920
This timeline shows the stale-while-revalidate pattern.

39
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.520
Users instantly see cache data, avoiding loading spinners,

40
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.400
while a background fetch swaps in fresh versions.

41
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.800
It also manages optimistic updates.

42
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.880
UI changes apply immediately.

43
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.960
If the server errors, the library automatically rolls back to the previous state.

44
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.800
As you fetch more endpoints,

45
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:58.040
maintaining a central query key factory pattern becomes a strict requirement to keep your cache organized.

46
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.600
Writing that extra organizational code is the necessary cost of doing business.

47
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:07.560
It prevents subtle, heart-to-track cache collision bugs across a massive application.

48
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.720
With server state handled, we need a lightweight tool for client state that solves

49
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.400
prop drilling and data persistence without adding heavy ceremony.

50
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.120
This diagram demonstrates ZewStand.

51
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.960
Instead of one giant object,

52
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.680
developers create independent cohesive slices of domain-specific data.

53
00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.480
By pointing selectors at specific slices,

54
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.160
UI components only subscribe to exact data they need.

55
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.400
If a cart updates, the user profile won't unnecessarily re-render.

56
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.040
The primary trade-off here is human error.

57
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.080
ZewStand does not enforce strict architecture rules,

58
00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:44.200
meaning your team needs strict discipline to keep files small

59
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.080
and avoid accidentally building a monolith anyway.

60
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.400
If you need extreme precision,

61
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.240
Jotai is the alternative.

62
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.000
It relies on atoms,

63
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.040
tiny individual units of state that nut perfectly to granular UI pieces.

64
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.400
This ensures that a re-render is limited only to the component reading that specific atom.

65
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.720
While Jotai minimizes re-renders,

66
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.840
creating an individual atom for every single variable creates atom proliferation,

67
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.640
in a large app, managing the sheer volume of them becomes a heavy organizational burden.

68
00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:19.560
This scrubbing timeline represents the heavy duty option,

69
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.000
Redux Toolkit.

70
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.600
It's built for massive applications needing intense observability

71
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.400
over a relentless stream of complex events.

72
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.240
Redux Toolkit provides time-travel debugging and a strict audit trail,

73
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.080
logging the exact history and sequence of every state mutation in your app.

74
00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.560
This look-up table shows how RTK query handles normalization.

75
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.040
Instead of duplicating data, entities are stored exactly once by ID.

76
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.880
With one source of truth,

77
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.800
updating a profile picture instantly syncs across a comment thread,

78
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.920
friend list, and settings page without leaving stale data.

79
00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.400
You pay a high premium and boiler code to get this architecture.

80
00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.960
It is only worth the cost if real-time,

81
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.440
multi-source syncing and strict predictability are non-negotiable business requirements.

82
00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.360
We have the tools.

83
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.000
Now we need a concrete framework to make the decision.

84
00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:10.920
This matrix is the blueprint.

85
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.200
For most apps, use TANstack for server state and zoo stand for client state.

86
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.560
Solos stick to you state.

87
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.120
Mid-level teams use the TANstack zoo stand hybrid for denormalized data.

88
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.240
Enterprise architects adopt Redux Toolkit for complex audits and strict normalization.

89
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.960
Sound architecture is never about hunting for a universal silver bullet.

90
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.400
It is about looking at the operational cost of a specific tool

91
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.040
and matching it precisely to the scale of your application.

